1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,420
 This question comes from Alan who asks if every human somehow simply disappeared from the face of the earth

2
00:00:05,420 --> 00:00:08,460
 How long would it be before the last artificial light source would go out?

3
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:17,040
 We'll start with the obvious most lights wouldn't last long because the major power grids would go down relatively quickly without people there would be less

4
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:21,580
 Demand for power, but our fridges and air conditioners and lava lamps would still be running fossil fuel plants

5
00:00:21,580 --> 00:00:23,580
 Which supply the vast majority of the world's electricity?

6
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:30,300
 Require a steady supply of fuel and their supply chains do involve people doing things as coal and oil plants started shutting down in the first

7
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:32,960
 Few hours other power sources would get hit with that extra load

8
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:39,000
 This kind of situation is difficult to handle even with human guidance and the result would be a rapid series of cascading failures leading to

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:40,960
 a blackout of all the major power grids

10
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:45,080
 Nuclear reactors of course don't require a steady supply of fuel one reactor operator

11
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:50,280
 I talked to said that if their core settled into low power mode it could continue running almost indefinitely a cube of uranium

12
00:00:50,440 --> 00:00:54,480
 Contains about six million times as much stored energy as a similar sized cube of coal

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,240
 Unfortunately, although there's enough fuel most nuclear reactors wouldn't keep running for long as soon as something went wrong

14
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:02,100
 The core would go into automatic shutdown

15
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:06,480
 Every part of a reactor control system is designed so that a failure causes it to rapidly shut down

16
00:01:06,520 --> 00:01:11,940
 This would happen quickly many things can trigger a shutdown, but the most likely culprit would be the failure of the power grid

17
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:15,760
 However, plenty of light comes from sources not tied to the major power grids

18
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:18,380
 Let's take a look at a few of those and when each one might turn off

19
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:22,920
 Many remote communities like those on far-flung islands get their power from diesel generators

20
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:27,480
 These can run until their tanks run out of fuel which in most cases could be anywhere from days to months

21
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:31,620
 Off-grid generating stations that don't need a human provided fuel supply would be in better shape

22
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:35,400
 Geothermal plants can run for a fair bit of time without human intervention

23
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:40,120
 According to the maintenance schedule for the Swarzengi Island geothermal plant in Iceland every six months

24
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:46,320
 The operators must change the gearbox oil and regrease all electric motors and couplings without humans to perform these sorts of maintenance procedures

25
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:49,480
 Some plants might run for a few years, but they'd also come to corrosion eventually

26
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:52,040
 Lights relying on wind power would last a bit longer

27
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:57,500
 Wind turbines are designed so that they don't need constant maintenance for the simple reason that there are a lot of them and they're a pain

28
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:03,560
 To climb the get sir wind turbine in Denmark was installed in the late 1950s and it generated power for 11 years without maintenance

29
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:10,000
 Modern turbines are typically rated to run for three years without servicing and there are no doubt some which would run for decades and one

30
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,580
 Of them would probably have at least a status LED in it somewhere

31
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:16,640
 Eventually, most of the wind turbines would be stopped by the same thing that would destroy the geothermal plants

32
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:18,640
 Their gearboxes would seize up

33
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:25,240
 Generators that convert falling water into electricity will also keep working an operator at the Hoover Dam once said that if everyone walked out the

34
00:02:25,240 --> 00:02:27,800
 Facility would continue to run on autopilot for several years

35
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:31,760
 So if the power grid is down all that electricity would have nowhere to go in the end

36
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:37,780
 The dam would probably succumb to clogged intakes or the same kind of mechanical failure that hit the wind turbines and geothermal plants

37
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:41,640
 Battery powered lights wouldn't fare much better and would all be off within a few dozen years

38
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:47,220
 Even without anything using their power batteries eventually self-discharge some types last longer than others

39
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:51,720
 But even batteries advertisers having long shelf lives typically only hold their charge for a decade or two

40
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:54,800
 Solar power is probably the most promising candidate

41
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:59,680
 There are many off-grid solar powered buildings weather stations and other remote infrastructure around the world

42
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:04,480
 Emergency call boxes often found along the side of the road in remote locations are frequently solar powered

43
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:08,440
 They usually have lights on them which provide illumination every night like wind turbines

44
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:14,280
 They're hard to service and so they're built to last for a long time solar panels will generally last as long as the electronics connected

45
00:03:14,280 --> 00:03:16,920
 To them and as long as the panels are kept free of dust and debris

46
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:20,040
 The wires and circuits will eventually succumb to corrosion

47
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:26,780
 but solar panels in a dry place with well-built electronics could easily continue providing power for a century if they're kept free of dust by

48
00:03:26,780 --> 00:03:33,800
 Occasional breezes or rain on the exposed panels if we follow a strict definition of lighting solar powered lights in remote locations could

49
00:03:33,960 --> 00:03:39,920
 Conceivably be the last surviving human light source, but there's another contender and it's a weird one spent nuclear fuel

50
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:43,760
 Contrary to popular portrayals radioactivity isn't usually visible

51
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:47,400
 Which is part of why we need warning signs around areas with radioactive materials or waste

52
00:03:47,720 --> 00:03:51,840
 Sure watch tiles used to be coated in small amounts of radium to help them glow in the dark

53
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:54,440
 But the glow didn't come from the radioactivity itself

54
00:03:54,440 --> 00:03:58,360
 It came from the phosphorus and paint on top of the radium which glows when it's irradiated

55
00:03:58,680 --> 00:04:02,220
 Once the phosphorus and paint breaks down the watch tiles are still radioactive

56
00:04:02,220 --> 00:04:09,400
 But no longer glow watch tiles, however are not our only radioactive light source when radioactive particles travel through materials like water or glass

57
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:11,640
 They emit light through a sort of optical sonic boom

58
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:16,620
 This light is called Cherenkov radiation and it's seen on the distinctive blue glow of nuclear reactor cores

59
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:23,760
 Some of our radioactive waste products such as cesium-137 are melted and mixed with glass which cools into a solid block before being wrapped in

60
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:28,640
 More shielding for transport and storage and in the dark these glass blocks glow blue

61
00:04:29,160 --> 00:04:36,560
 cesium-137 has a half-life of 30 years which means that two centuries later the blocks will still be glowing with 1% of their original radioactivity

62
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:41,620
 Since the color of the light depends only on the particle decay energy and not the amount of radiation

63
00:04:41,620 --> 00:04:43,180
 It will fade in brightness over time

64
00:04:43,180 --> 00:04:46,640
 But keep that same blue color and thus we arrive at our answer

65
00:04:46,920 --> 00:04:51,960
 Centuries from now deep in concrete vaults the light from our most toxic waste will still be shining

66
00:04:59,160 --> 00:05:01,740
 (gentle music)

